// Shop content — what the player can buy, and what it costs (GAMEPLAY.md
// §1's core loop and §4's "equipment purchases *are* the tech tree"). Data,
// never hardcoded in sim_core, exactly like `recipes.ron` and
// `machines.ron`; the loader is `sim_core::sim::shop::ShopCatalog`.
//
// `schema_version` follows ARCHITECTURE.md §4's data-file versioning rule:
// the loader rejects a mismatched version rather than silently misreading a
// changed shape, the same way the other two data files' loaders do.
//
// WHAT THIS FILE IS FOR. GAMEPLAY.md §1 is explicit that there is no
// separate currency or tech-point resource: "money buys equipment,
// equipment unlocks sauces, sauces satisfy contracts, contracts pay money."
// This file is the "money buys equipment" half of that sentence made
// concrete — a catalog of every buildable kind together with its price in
// the same money `Economy` already counts. Buying a kind credits one to the
// player's owned count of it, and placing one spends it.
//
// WHAT AN OWNED COUNT MEANS (updated by the "Real Placement" epic). When
// this file first landed, buying something credited a count nothing read:
// the whole production line existed unconditionally from tick 0 whether or
// not anything had been purchased. That is no longer true. `World::new` now
// builds *nothing*, and every belt tile, pipe tile, machine and building in
// a world is something a `purchase` paid for and a `place` put down — so an
// owned count is unplaced inventory, `place` spends one and `remove` credits
// one back. GAMEPLAY.md §4's "equipment purchases *are* the tech tree" is a
// statement about the simulation now, not an intention.
//
// KIND IDS. An `id` here is a *buildable kind* id, which is a superset of
// `data/machines.ron`'s machine kinds: every kind that file defines appears
// here (the loader's `validate_covers` checks exactly that, so adding a
// machine kind without pricing it is an error rather than a thing the
// player can never buy), plus the two pieces of infrastructure that are not
// machines at all — a belt tile and a pipe tile. Those two, and the splitter
// that sits between them in a line, are priced *per tile*, because that is
// the unit a player places them in; everything else is priced per building.
//
// PRICES ARE PLACEHOLDERS. GAMEPLAY.md §10 puts exact numeric balance
// ("ingredient costs, contract payouts, equipment prices, wage rates")
// explicitly in a future balancing pass, not here, and this project is
// pre-alpha. What these numbers *do* encode is a deliberate shape, so the
// catalog reads sensibly even before it is balanced:
//
//   1. Infrastructure is cheap enough to lay by the dozen; a building costs
//      an order of magnitude more than a tile of belt.
//   2. A building's price tracks how much machine it is — the same instinct
//      `machines.ron` uses to decide footprints. The 2x2 cooking vat that
//      runs the chain's longest process is the most expensive thing here;
//      the crusher, the simplest machine on the line, is the cheapest.
//   3. Everything is scaled against the one economic number that already
//      exists: the shipped board's middle contract pays 50 per delivery. So
//      a belt tile is small change, a mid-chain machine is a couple of
//      deliveries, and the cooking vat is a genuine save-up.
//   4. A new game starts with `economy.rs`'s STARTING_MONEY (3000), and
//      against these prices the whole shipped five-stage line comes to well
//      under half of it — so a new player can build all of it outright and
//      still have two more lines' worth left over, which also has to cover
//      the raw ingredients it runs on (`data/ingredients.ron`, priced
//      separately because an ingredient is consumed and a machine is not).
//      That margin is the point: money
//      only arrives through contract deliveries, deliveries need a line
//      that reaches the dock, so a starting balance below the bill closes
//      nothing (which is what the previous 200 did). The two numbers are
//      balanced against each other, so changing prices here means
//      revisiting that constant — `sim_core/tests/shop.rs` pins the
//      relationship rather than either literal, and will fail if a price
//      change breaks it.
//
// Order matters only for presentation: the loader preserves this file's
// order and `shop_snapshot_json` reports the catalog in it, so this is the
// order a build menu lists things in. It runs infrastructure first, then the
// two feed-end buffers, then the chain's machines in chain order, then the
// delivery dock — which has no recipe, so it is placed by its POSITION in
// the chain (the tail) rather than by recipe order.
(
    schema_version: 1,
    entries: [
        // --- Infrastructure, priced per tile ---
        ShopEntry(
            id: "belt",
            price: 5,
        ),
        // Slightly dearer than a belt tile: a pipe run is stainless and
        // carries a fluid (UI_UX.md §2b point 6), where a belt is rubber.
        ShopEntry(
            id: "pipe",
            price: 8,
        ),
        // Epic B's conveyor splitter — one belt in, two belts out. Listed
        // with the infrastructure rather than with the buildings because
        // that is what it is: a tile you drop into a line, the same size and
        // the same speed as the belt tile it stands in place of (it holds
        // what it carries in an ordinary one-tile belt segment — see
        // `sim_core/src/sim/splitter.rs`).
        //
        // Five belt tiles' worth, which is a real premium over the 5 a plain
        // tile costs and still an order of magnitude under any building, per
        // shape rule 1 above. The premium is meant to be felt but not saved
        // up for: a splitter is a mechanism (it holds product and chooses
        // where each unit goes) where a belt is a surface, and half the
        // point of building one is that it is cheaper than the second
        // production line it lets you feed. Priced above the pipe for the
        // same reason the pipe is priced above the belt.
        ShopEntry(
            id: "splitter",
            price: 25,
        ),

        // --- Buffers: placed buildings with no recipe (see machines.ron) ---
        ShopEntry(
            id: "hopper",
            price: 40,
        ),
        // Dearer than the hopper for the same reason a pipe is dearer than
        // a belt — a pressure vessel is more than a bin.
        ShopEntry(
            id: "fluid_tank",
            price: 60,
        ),

        // --- Chain machines, in `data/recipes.ron` order ---
        // The line's simplest machine (a hopper and a screw) and its
        // cheapest.
        ShopEntry(
            id: "crusher",
            price: 75,
        ),
        ShopEntry(
            id: "mixer",
            price: 100,
        ),
        // The most expensive thing in the catalog, on purpose: the biggest
        // machine on the line, running the chain's longest process.
        // GAMEPLAY.md §4 treats the cooker/kettle as a real capital
        // purchase, and this is it. (It is no longer the only 2x2 kind —
        // the delivery dock grew to 2x2 in the 2026-09-09 tuning pass — but
        // it is still the only 2x2 kind that *does* anything, which is what
        // this price is for.)
        ShopEntry(
            id: "cooking_vat",
            price: 200,
        ),
        // GAMEPLAY.md §4 groups bottling with the reduction step as one
        // equipment tier, so it is priced in the vat's neighbourhood rather
        // than with the cheap front end.
        ShopEntry(
            id: "bottler",
            price: 120,
        ),
        ShopEntry(
            id: "packer",
            price: 90,
        ),

        // --- The chain's exit, placed by position rather than by recipe ---
        // The delivery dock has no recipe at all (like the hopper and the
        // fluid tank), so it cannot be placed in "recipes.ron order" the way
        // every entry above it is. It is listed last because that is where
        // it stands on the floor: the packer's boxes run down one more belt
        // and into it, and it ships them into the shared delivery pool every
        // tick without the player doing anything.
        //
        // Priced between the two other no-recipe buffers and the cheap
        // machines: a loading bay with a conveyor head is more than the
        // hopper's bin and less than any of the processing equipment, and
        // it is the one building whose value is that the line does not need
        // watching once it exists.
        //
        // **The 2026-09-09 tuning pass grew the dock to 2x2 and left this
        // number alone, on purpose.** Price here tracks what a building
        // does for the player, not how many tiles it claims: only the
        // infrastructure group above is priced per tile, and the 2x2
        // cooking vat costs 200 for the process it runs rather than for its
        // four tiles. A dock does no work at all — it is still exactly one
        // door out of the factory, however much apron stands around it —
        // and the extra tiles are already a real cost in the only currency
        // a factory-builder actually feels here, which is floor space.
        ShopEntry(
            id: "dock",
            price: 70,
        ),
    ],
)
